{"caseId":"OD2-HO-CONTRA-2","methodChoice":"Collected 2 of 2 items. X15 first: it compares night and day shifts and suppliers, which tests the staffing-cut explanation directly. Then X13: independent trailer logs, to test whether Haverlow loads arrived warm. I skipped the credit-side items because they affect only about 15% of the rise and would not change the lead action.","answers":[{"itemId":"Q1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":90,"evidenceIds":["X13","X15","E4","E3"]},{"itemId":"Q2","answer":"supported","confidence":95,"evidenceIds":["X15","X13","E3","E5"]},{"itemId":"Q3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":92,"evidenceIds":["E6","X15"]},{"itemId":"Q4","answer":"contradicted","confidence":90,"evidenceIds":["X13","E3","X15"]},{"itemId":"Q5","answer":"unknown","confidence":62,"evidenceIds":["E7","E1","X15"]},{"itemId":"Q6","answer":"causal","confidence":90,"evidenceIds":["E5","X15"]},{"itemId":"Q7","answer":"causal","confidence":86,"evidenceIds":["X15","X13","E3"]},{"itemId":"Q8","answer":"contradicted","confidence":86,"evidenceIds":["X13","X15","E4"]},{"itemId":"Q9","answer":"association-only","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E7","E1"]},{"itemId":"Q10","answer":"harmful","confidence":90,"evidenceIds":["X13","X15","E4"]},{"itemId":"Q11","answer":"preferred","confidence":90,"evidenceIds":["X15","E5","X13","E1"]},{"itemId":"Q12","answer":"premature","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["E7","E1"]},{"itemId":"Q13","answer":"acceptable","confidence":72,"evidenceIds":["E7","X15"]},{"itemId":"Q14","answer":"harmful","confidence":95,"evidenceIds":["X15","E3","X13"]},{"itemId":"Q15","answer":"sufficient","confidence":88,"evidenceIds":["X15","X13","E5","E1"]}],"brief":"Most of the jump comes from inside the DC, not from Haverlow. Since the night receiving team was cut from six to four on 27 July, night-received chilled pallets wait a median 3h52 (previously 44 minutes) on an unrefrigerated dock averaging 15–17°C before reaching the chambers. Night write-offs rose from 0.6% to 2.9%; day shift stayed near 0.5%; the night rate is the same for Haverlow, Pellridge and own-label. The trailers' own uneditable logs show Haverlow product at 2.4–3.5°C when doors opened; the 'warm' receipt probes were recorded about 3.5 hours after unloading. Chambers held temperature. DC-stage write-offs are up about £15,700 a week against a £2,300 weekly labour saving.

I'm about 90% sure. Less certain is the £2,700 weekly rise in customer credits: Corriebank's easier claims may explain some, real dock-delay spoilage may explain the rest; the evidence can't yet split them.

Next: restore the night team, set a maximum dock-to-chamber time, and track it weekly from scan data. Suspend the Haverlow chargeback and volume move; keep the receiving probe. Separately, get Corriebank's claim-level data and check a sample before changing credit terms."}
